--- the same crash, with durability='sync' ---
recovered state: 1 step(s), 6 messages, next node ('think',)
the default run lost the finished act and ran it again; the sync run kept it

The first process died. It did not save anything on the way out, it did not
catch a signal, and it did not finish. What had been checkpointed survived. By
default a checkpoint is stored while the next step starts, so the step that had
just finished can be lost with the process; with durability='sync' it is stored
before the next step begins, at the cost of waiting for the write.

Chapter 17's ninety lines cannot do this, and adding it is not a small change:
the loop has to stop being a `while` and start being a sequence of transitions
over serialisable state. That is the same thing as writing the framework.
